From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: detect the rest of a late CPU's errata during boot, not just Spectre-v2

arm64/0003 detects Spectre-v2 during boot when the device tree declares a
Cortex-A72 that will only be hotplugged at ~240 s, so that the alternatives are
patched before it arrives and its cpu_enable() does something real. Its own
commit message then says the technique stops there, because v3a, v4 and BHB
"choose their mitigation per CPU" and so hit issue #60's option 2, "teach the
mitigation code to late-patch a per-CPU vector table".

That is wrong. All five remaining capabilities go the same way as v2, and this
patch does them. Three things had to be understood first.

1. THE BHB VECTOR TABLES ARE PRE-BUILT AND SHARED, NOT PER-CPU.

entry.S generates four complete tables at ASSEMBLY time -- BHB_MITIGATION_NONE,
LOOP, FW, INSN -- with assembler .if, not runtime alternatives.
this_cpu_set_vectors() only points VBAR_EL1 at one of them, and a late CPU does
that itself, correctly. Nothing is per-CPU except the choice.

2. WHAT IS PATCHED AT BOOT IS AN IMMEDIATE COMPUTED FROM THE BOOT CPUS.

Inside the shared LOOP table, spectre_bhb_patch_loop_iter() writes the loop
count from spectre_bhb_loop_affected(SCOPE_SYSTEM) -- and that is a static
accumulator, not a survey:

	if (scope == SCOPE_LOCAL_CPU) {
		...			/* MIDR_CORTEX_A72 is in the k=8 list */
		max_bhb_k = max(max_bhb_k, k);
	} else {
		k = max_bhb_k;		/* only what the boot CPUs contributed */
	}

With no A72 at boot that immediate is patched to zero, and the loop it feeds is

	mov	x30, #0
     1:	b	. + 4
	subs	x30, x30, #1
	b.ne	1b

i.e. 2^64 iterations. THAT is why issue #60 records an A72 dying "within ~280 ms
of the scheduler placing the first userspace task on it": its enable had pointed
VBAR_EL1 at that table and the first exception from EL0 never came back. The
diagnosis in #60 and in arm64/0001 -- "the alternatives are still NOPs" -- named
the wrong instruction. It is one alternative, patched with a number derived from
a set of CPUs that did not include the only CPU that would ever execute it.

3. THE PER-CPU GUARDS ARE THE MECHANISM, NOT THE OBSTACLE.

spectre_bhb_enable_mitigation() opens with

	if (!is_spectre_bhb_affected(entry, SCOPE_LOCAL_CPU))
		return;

and arm64/0003's message reads that as fatal: force the detection and the A53s
"detect it and then decline to install anything". They do decline -- and that is
exactly what we want. It is why this costs the eight A53s nothing. The bug is
only that the same function is both the capability's .matches and the enable's
guard, so forcing one forces the other. Split them, and an unaffected boot CPU
detects the capability on the A72's behalf, arranges what the A72 will need, and
stays on the BHB_MITIGATION_NONE vectors itself.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES

* Exports arm64_will_have_late_a72() (was static system_will_have_late_a72) so
  cpu_errata.c can use it too, and documents where it is safe to call: the
  device-tree walk happens during capability detection and everything later,
  including alternative callbacks running under stop_machine, reads the cache.

* Splits an honest local-CPU check out of has_spectre_v3a() and
  is_spectre_bhb_affected(), and uses it in the two enable paths.
  spectre_v3a_enable_mitigation()'s this_cpu_has_cap(ARM64_SPECTRE_V3A) meant
  precisely "is the CPU I am on affected" and stops meaning it the moment the
  capability can be forced; cpu_has_spectre_v3a() means it again.

* Folds the declared A72's k = 8 into spectre_bhb_loop_affected(SCOPE_SYSTEM),
  which is the fix for (2).

* Sets BHB_LOOP in system_bhb_mitigations at boot. The EL1 tables do not need
  it -- their loop is unconditional -- but KVM's hyp vectors do, and nothing
  re-patches those at 240 s.

* Seeds spectre_v2_state, spectre_v4_state and spectre_bhb_state at boot with
  what the A72 will report -- INCLUDING when that answer is SPECTRE_VULNERABLE,
  which is the case here. update_mitigation_state() WARNs rather than advance
  the state after finalisation, so a late CPU that is the FIRST affected CPU can
  never record its own verdict; it would leave sysfs saying "Not affected" for a
  machine that had just stopped being that, which is the exact failure
  arm64/0002 was written to stop. Measured: without the VULNERABLE half of this,
  build #109 took that WARN on both A72s at 243 s.

* Adds is_affected_midr_range_list_or_late_a72() for the two errata, whose whole
  effect IS the capability being set (neither has a .cpu_enable). 1742098 is not
  a speculation mitigation at all but a correctness bug: a 32-bit task is being
  told the aarch32 AES instructions work, and the scheduler is free to put it on
  an A72 where they do not.

WHAT IT MEASURES, WHICH IS NOT WHAT #60 EXPECTED

Build #109 is the first kernel on this device to enable all six, and the first
therefore to report what the machine actually is:

	spectre_v2:        Vulnerable
	spec_store_bypass: Vulnerable

not the "Not affected" of every kernel before arm64/0002, nor the "late CPU
permitted without mitigation" of #101..#106. THE FIRMWARE HAS NO SPECTRE
WORKAROUNDS AT ALL:

	psci: PSCIv0.2 detected in firmware.

psci_probe() only calls psci_init_smccc() for PSCI >= 1.0, so SMCCC 1.1 is never
negotiated, arm_smccc_1_1_get_conduit() is SMCCC_CONDUIT_NONE, and every
ARM_SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1/_2/_3 discovery returns NOT_SUPPORTED. A Cortex-A72
has no CSV2 and no SSBS, so on this device:

	Spectre-v2   unmitigable -- no hardware, no firmware
	Spectre-v4   unmitigable -- no SSBS, no firmware
	Spectre-BHB  the k=8 loop needs no firmware, but upstream declines it
		     while v2 is vulnerable ("No point mitigating Spectre-BHB
		     alone") and that judgement is not ours to overturn
	Spectre-v3a  ENABLED (KVM hyp vector slot)
	1742098      ENABLED (aarch32 AES hwcap withdrawn)
	1165522/1319367/1530923   ENABLED (KVM hyp ISBs)

So #60's stated goal -- mitigate the late A72s -- is reached for three of six,
and the other three are blocked by firmware that predates the mitigations, which
is not something any amount of late-CPU work in the kernel can reach. The
handoff's "you are not blocked on firmware" was inferred from has_spectre_v2()
returning true on cpu8; that function returns true for NOT_SUPPORTED as well as
for SUCCESS, so it never said what it was read as saying.

There is a second, independent benefit to getting v2 detected even though it
cannot be mitigated: spectre_v2_state is now VULNERABLE at boot, so upstream's
own guard refuses the BHB vectors to the A72, and the 2^64-iteration loop of (2)
can no longer be reached even if the k fold were removed.

WHAT IT COSTS

  ARM64_SPECTRE_V2	 arm64_apply_bp_hardening(), which is on the EL0 fault
			 paths AND on the switch_mm fast path -- i.e. every
			 context switch between address spaces, not just an ASID
			 rollover. The A53s are in the v2 safe list, so
			 spectre_v2_enable_mitigation() installs no callback on
			 them and the added work is a per-CPU load and a NULL
			 test; but it is on a hot path and is the only cost here
			 that is.
  ARM64_SPECTRE_V4	 nothing outside cpu_errata.c. The entry-time SMC is
			 gated on the per-CPU arm64_ssbd_callback_required, set
			 only on a CPU that really is affected.
  ARM64_SPECTRE_V3A	 KVM only.
  ARM64_SPECTRE_BHB	 the A53s stay on the BHB_MITIGATION_NONE vectors, so
			 nothing at EL1. If BHB_LOOP is set (it is not on this
			 device, see above) the hyp vectors are SHARED, so every
			 core would run the k=8 loop on guest exception entry --
			 unavoidable, what upstream does anyway, and free unless
			 a guest is running.
  ARM64_WORKAROUND_SPECULATIVE_AT   KVM only.
  ARM64_WORKAROUND_1742098          withdraws COMPAT_HWCAP2_AES.

arm64/0003's message claims "the eight A53s pay ... an SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1
call on exception entry from EL0". They do not, and never did: MIDR_CORTEX_A53
is in spectre_v2_safe_list, so spectre_v2_enable_mitigation() takes the
hw-state-UNAFFECTED path and never reaches install_bp_hardening_cb(). Its -6.4%
for A53-L was DVFS noise across two boots -- #103's policy4 sat at 962 MHz and
#104's at 1209 MHz, and the SLOWER clock scored HIGHER.

MEASURED, #110 against #106, three bench runs each on one boot per build
(scripts/gemini-bench.sh; 04-docs/bench/*-baseline-106-r[1-3].txt against
*-mitigated-110-r[1-6].txt):

  metric                        #106 (off)    #110 (all six on)    delta
  cpu0 stint ms           1474 [1464-1479] n=3      1478 [1474-1481] n=6    +0.26%
  cpu4 stint ms           1453 [1453-1453] n=3      1456 [1455-1458] n=6    +0.17%
  cpu8 stint ms            903 [902-904]   n=3       900 [891-904]   n=6    -0.31%
  unpinned stint ms        903 [888-909]  n=15       904 [889-909]  n=30    +0.02%
  spawn us                2652 [2590-2690] n=6      2636 [2541-2700] n=12   -0.61%
  pingpong us               50 [44-57]     n=6        56 [43-64]     n=12  +12.97%
  A53 memcpy MB/s         1327 [1311-1355] n=3      1311 [1246-1362] n=6    -1.16%
  A72 memcpy MB/s         3641 [3602-3683] n=3      3586 [3354-3711] n=6    -1.52%
  4-way wall ms           1247 [1246-1249] n=3      1250 [1243-1258] n=6    +0.20%

Single-thread compute is the tightest metric this harness has -- cpu4 gave
1453/1453/1453 ms on three consecutive baseline runs -- and it moves +0.17%.
Spawn, 4-way and memcpy are inside their own spread. So: no measurable cost, as
the grep above predicts, and nothing like the -6.4% in the record.

ONE METRIC IS NOT EXPLAINED. Unpinned pingpong's mean moves +13%, on ranges that
overlap (43.5-57.1 against 43.2-64.0) and with the widest run-to-run spread in
the suite. It is the only unpinned latency measure, so it is also the one most
exposed to scheduler placement. The only new per-context-switch work is the
per-CPU load and NULL test above, which cannot be 7 us. Recorded rather than
explained away; re-measure with more runs if it ever matters.

Still default n. #60 records the unmitigated state as accepted, and reversing an
accepted trade is the owner's call, not a default.

